% Abstract (Do not insert blank lines, i.e. \\) 
{% Problem (relevance, importance)
The severity of natural disasters is increasing every year, having an impact on many people's lives. During the response phase of disasters, airports are important hubs where relief aid arrives while people need to be evacuated out of there. However, the airport often forms a bottleneck in these relief operations, because of the sudden need for increased capacity. 
% Research gap
Limited research is done on the operational side of airport disaster management. Experts identify the main problems as first the asymmetry of information between the airport and the incoming flights, and second the lack of resources.
% Main question/objective
The goal of this research is to gain understanding of the effects of incomplete knowledge of incoming flights with different resource allocation strategies, on the performance of the cargo handling operations in an airport after a natural disaster event.
% Summarise methodology in 2 sentences
An agent-based model is created, where realistic offloading strategies with different degrees of information uncertainty are implemented. Model's calibration and verification are performed with experts in the field.
% Key results
The model performance is measured by the average turn-around time, which can be split in offloading time, boarding time and the cumulative waiting times. The results show that the effects of one unplanned aircraft are negligible. However, all waiting times increase with the more arriving unplanned aircraft.}

