Using the matches determined by the MHD method, we can construct a scaling relation between the velocity dispersion of the optical galaxies and the X-ray luminosity. This scaling relation takes the form of the power law shown in equation \ref{eq:scaling_relation}.  Figures \ref{fig:velD_Lx_180} and \ref{fig:velD_Lx_195} present the scaling relation for the catalogs v180 and v195 from \cite{Tempel2014}, respectively. The posteriors of the power law fits are shown in figures \ref{fig:triangle_plot_180} and \ref{fig:triangle_plot_195}, respectively. For X-ray/optical matches with center distances greater than 200 kpc, the posteriors resemble those of the full catalog. However, matches with centers less than 200 kpc exhibit a slightly higher variance in the posteriors of the parameters. 

\begin{equation}
    \ln{\sigma\; \mathrm{km}^{-1} \mathrm{s} } = \beta \ln{\left(\frac{L_x}{10^{43}\; \mathrm{ergs\; s}^{-1}}\right) + N(\alpha,\sigma')}
    \label{eq:scaling_relation}
\end{equation}

Additionally, we applied the v195 MHD cutoff to each group in the complete {galaxy} catalog \cite{Tempel2014} as an alternative way to determine the membership of the cluster and recalculate the velocity dispersion measurements. Membership was determined by calculating the distance of each galaxy from the rest of the group and removing it if the distance exceeded the v195 MHD cutoff. We have recomputed the median value of right ascension and declination for the remaining group members and performed the cleaning for outliers using the {\it Clean} {algorithm} \citep{Mamon2013}.

