\textbf{Results.} Examples of the bias-dependent noise are shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:2}(a) for an LD-Ta VNJ with $L=8$~nm. The dependence is linear at large $V_b$ and saturates at small $V_B$, which is well-approximated by Eq.~(\ref{eq:SN_general}) [solid lines in Fig.~\ref{fig:2}(a)]. The data do not exhibit any downcurving expected for the e-ph interaction~\cite{SNDiffusive,Henny1997}. The temperature-dependent Fano factors and thermal broadening determined from the fitting are shown in Figs.~\ref{fig:2}(b),(c). At $T=4.2$~K, they are close to $\sqrt{3}/4\approx0.43$ and $2/\sqrt{3}\approx1.15$, respectively, expected for the hot-electron regime, suggesting a thermalized electron state due to strong e-e interactions. 

The Fano factor decreases with increasing $T$, Fig.~\ref{fig:2}(b). A similar suppression in nanowires of $\beta$-Ta~\cite{Szurek2025} and heavy fermion ``strange" metals~\cite{chen2023shot} was attributed to a correlated electron liquid. However, if electrons are thermalized at $T=4.2$~K, they are also likely thermalized at higher $T$, so noise contains information only about the effective electron temperature $T_e$. In this case, the decrease of $F$ indicates that $T_e$ is reduced by an additional dissipation channel, which can be described by the diffusion equation~\cite{PhysRevB.49.5942,Henny1997}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:diffusion}
\sigma\left(\frac{ V_B}{L}\right)^2=-\frac{\partial}{\partial x}\left(\frac{\kappa_e\partial T_e}{\partial x}\right)+f,    
\end{equation}
where $\sigma$ is conductivity, $\kappa_e$ is the electron thermal conductivity, and $f(T_e,T)$ is the rate of dissipation into the additional channel. For negligible $f$, Wiedemann-Franz law $\kappa_e=\sigma T L_0$ leads to a linear dependence of noise on bias at $V_B>V_{th}$. Here, $L_0$ is the Lorenz number. A finite $f$ typically leads to a nonlinear dependence. For instance, for e-ph coupling described by $f\propto(T^5_e-T^5)$, one obtains $S_V\propto V_B^{2/5}$ at $T_e\gg T$~\cite{SNDiffusive}. To reproduce the linear $S_V(V_B)$ observed at $V>V_{th}$, one can empirically approximate $f$ by the same form as the electron contribution, $f(T_e,T)=-\frac{f_0}{2}\partial^2 T^2_e/\partial x^2$, where $f_0$ is a function of only temperature $T$, 
giving
\begin{equation}\label{eq:fano_diss}
F(T)=\frac{\pi k_B}{4e\sqrt{L_0+f_0(T)}}.
\end{equation}

This mechanism describes suppressed $F$, but also increased thermal broadening, $B=2e\sqrt{L_0+f_0}/\pi k_B$. Instead, we find that $B$ is approximately proportional to $F$, Fig.~\ref{fig:2}(d). This indicates that the dependence on bias is not fully captured by this form of $f$, which may be caused by the non-equilibrium dissipation effects that can qualitatively change the bias dependence~\cite{Chen2020}. 
%For dissipation via phonons, this may be associated with the non-equilibrium phonon distribution driven by e-ph interaction, which cannot be described by an effective temperature~\cite{Chen2020}. 
Despite this limitation of the proposed approximation, it provides a useful framework for the analysis of SN, as shown below.

\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{Fig3.pdf}
\caption{\label{fig:3} (a) $F$ vs $T$ for two $\beta$-Ta nanowires with the same length $L=500$ nm and cross-section areas, but different contact area with the substrate as determined by thickness. Curves: fittings as described in the text. (b), (c)  Fitting parameters in the dependence $f_0=a+bT^3$ vs $L$, for VNJs and nanowires as labeled. Lines are guides for the eye. To maximize the precision of $a$, fitting was performed for $T\leq10$~K. Inset in (b): nanowire schematic. (d) $F$ vs $L$ at $T = 4.2$~K for LD-Ta VNJs ($L\leq20$~nm) and $\beta$-Ta nanowires ($L\geq 100$ nm).}
\end{figure}

The dissipation leading to suppressed $F$ and described by $f(T,T_e)$ can be mediated by phonons or electronic degrees of freedom such as spin or collective excitations that do not contribute to charge transport~\cite{chen2023shot}. To elucidate the possible phonon contribution, we also studied nanowires of pure $\beta$-Ta deposited directly on sapphire substrates and patterned by e-beam lithography [inset in Fig.~\ref{fig:3}(b)]. Similarly to VNJs, the dependence of noise on bias is well-approximated by Eq.~(\ref{eq:SN_general}) [see SM~\cite{supp}]. Figure~\ref{fig:3}(a) shows $F(T)$ for two nanowires with the same length $L=500$~nm and cross-section areas but different aspect ratios: thickness $d=10$~nm, width $w=750$~nm for one nanowire, and $d=20$~nm, $w=375$~nm for the other. Both electrical and thermal transport characteristics of the two nanowires themselves were similar, but the contact areas with the substrate differed by a factor of $2$. For the wire with the larger contact area, $F$ is significantly smaller at $4.2$~K and more rapidly decreases with decreasing $T$, as expected for the phonon effects due to more efficient dissipation into the substrate.

The function $f_0(T)$ obtained from the temperature dependence of the Fano factor is well approximated by the empirical relation $f_0(T)=a+bT^3$ for all the studied structures, as illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:2}(a) for a VNJ, and in Fig.~\ref{fig:3}(a) for two nanowires. A finite $a>0$ reflects suppression of $F$ due to the additional dissipation in the limit $T\to0$, while $b>0$ describes its enhancement with increasing $T$. Both $a$ and $b$ are significantly larger for nanowires than VNJs, consistent with additional dissipation into the substrate. Two important conclusions follow from these dependences. First, a large value of $a$ for nanowires implies that SN is suppressed even in the limit $T\to0$, contrary to the expectation for weak e-ph coupling in Fermi liquids~\cite{SNDiffusive,stierbach1996hotElectron}. Second, the small value of $b$ in VNJs shows that thermal effects on $F$ are small at $T=4.2$~K. For instance, for the $L=8$~nm VNJ, $b=1.3\times10-11\,V^2/K^5$ gives $F(T=0)$ only $4\%$ larger than $F(4.2\,K)$. We note that fitting gives a small $a<0$ for both $L=8$~nm and $20$ nm, signifying that at $T\to0$ SN is slightly enhanced compared to the hot-electron regime. This may be an indication of electron pairing, as discussed below, but the difference is too small for a definitive conclusion. We also note that for $L = 4$ nm, both $a$ and $b$ subtantially deviate from the values for larger $L$, indicating a change in the transport regime.

\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{Fig4.pdf}
\caption{\label{fig:4}  
(a),(b) Schematics of the hopping mechanism mediated by phonons (a) and e-e interaction (b). (c) $\rho$ vs $T$ for $\beta-Ta$ and a series of nitrogen-doped Ta films, with fittings with the model of hopping mediated by the e-e interaction, as discussed in the text. (d) Ratio of e-e interaction-mediated to phonon mediated hopping rates at $T=300$ determined from the fittings in (c). Line is a guide for the eye.}
\end{figure}

Based on these observations, we can infer the relevant transport regimes from the noise measurements at $T=4.2$~K, Fig.~\ref{fig:4}(a). The Fano factor for VNJs with $L=8$~nm and $20$~nm is close to $F=\sqrt{3}/4$ expected for thermalized electrons. For $L=4$~nm, it approaches $F=1/3$ expected for single-electron diffusion, suggesting that $4\,nm\lesssim l_{e-e}\lesssim 8$~nm, $l_{e-ph}>20$~nm at $4.2$~K. Finally, the Fano factor is suppressed in nanowires due to e-ph interaction. The thermal broadening was small in all the samples, $B<1/F$, eliminating phonon-mediated hopping as the transport mechanism [see SM for details~\cite{supp}]. The dependence of $F(L)$ is similar to that in noble-metal wires~\cite{stierbach1996hotElectron}, but with at least three orders of magnitude smaller characteristic lengths. %A very short $l_{e-e}<8$~nm indicates strong e-e interactions whose implications for the ``failed" insulator state are discussed below.

%We address the effects of disorder on the transport mechanism by analyzing noise in VNJs based on Ta deposited at nitrogen pressure $1\times10^-4$~Torr, which we label HD-Ta. Higher nitrogen doping resulted in doubling of both the carrier concentration and resistivity, Fig.~\ref{fig:1}(a). The estimated effective Drude mfp $l<0.1$~nm is unphysically small. However, the small thermal broadening factors $B=1.0-1.8$ (see SM) and the lack of resistivity divergence [Fig.~\ref{fig:1}(a)] exclude localization. Instead, the values of the Fano factor at $T=4.2$~K are close to the $F=1/3$ expected for single-electron diffusion for all three values of $L$, suggesting that electron thermalization is suppressed by disorder.  

\textbf{Discussion.} The resistivity $\rho$, the Hall coefficient of $\beta$-Ta and LD-Ta [Fig.~\ref{fig:1}] [see also SM~\cite{supp} for ellipsometry] provide an estimate for the upper limit of the effective Drude mean free path (mfp) $l\lesssim~0.3$~nm, close to the inter-atomic spacing $a\approx0.28$~nm. This unphysically small value, known as the Ioffe-Regel limit, is generally attributed to the electron localization~\cite{Hussey2004}. The crystal structure of $\beta$-Ta facilitates localization, as follows. Its unit cell contains $30$ inequivalent Ta atoms~\cite{Magnuson2019}, and nitrogen doping results in further variations of the environment of each atomic site, so the states quasi-localized at different sites are generally expected to have different energies. NTCR can be then be explained by hopping between these states analogous to Mott's hopping between impurity states in semiconductors~\cite{Liu2010}. %Both $\rho$ and NTCR increase with nitrogen doping, as expected due to increasing energy differences among the localized states. 
However, this does not explain the lack of resistivity divergence expected for the hopping transport, or the absence of signatures of hopping transport in SN measurements.

We now show that these properties are consistent with short electron lifetime due to strong e-e interactions, as inferred from the short thermalization length $l_{th}<8$~nm. Bloch electrons can be described by the Green's function $G(\mathbf{k},E) = (E - \epsilon(k) -\Sigma)^{-1}$, where $E$ is energy relative to Fermi surface, $\mathbf{k}$ is the wavevector, $\epsilon(k)$ is the bare single-particle energy, and $\Sigma(E,\mathbf{k})=\Sigma'-i\gamma$ is the self-energy determined by e-e interactions~\cite{Camacho2022,GreenFunc,Yong2008,Keyan2010}. In Fermi liquids, the lifetime $\tau=\hbar/\gamma$ diverges at $E\to0$ due to the constraints imposed by momentum conservation~\cite{RevModPhys.94.041002}. For quasi-localized electrons, interactions are not constrained by momentum conservation, so lifetime does not necessarily diverge at small $E$. Thus, ``failed" insulators may realize an extreme limit of energy-independent relaxation, the other extreme being Fermi liquids (Ohmic relaxation $\tau\propto E^2$), with `strange" metals characterized by Planckian relaxation ($\tau\propto E$) representing an intermediate case.

%We use diffusive approximation for an order-of-magnitude estimate $\tau=l_{th}^2/4D\sim10^{-13}$~s, where $D=v_F l\sim 10^{-4}$~m$^2$/s is the diffusion coefficient, $v_F\sim 10^6$~m/s is the Fermi velocity. The corresponding energy broadening is $\Delta E=\hbar/2\tau\sim3$~meV, in the same range as thermal phonon energies. This under-estimates the broadening inferred from the analysis below, likely due to the inadequacy of diffusion approximation.

The rate of phonon-assisted hopping between localized states $i$, $j$ with energies $\epsilon_j>\epsilon_i$ assisted by thermal phonons with populations $N(\omega)=(e^{\hbar\omega/k_BT}-1)^{-1}$ is~\cite{Vukmirovic2010,Damascelli2003,bruus2004}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:phonon-assist}
\Gamma_{ph,i\to j} = \frac{2\pi}{\hbar}\sum_{\mathbf{k},E}|M_{ij}|^2N(\omega(k))A_i(E)A_j(E+\hbar\omega),
\end{equation}
where $M_{ij}=\langle i,1_k|H_{e-ph}|j,0_k\rangle$ is the matrix element of the e-ph coupling Hamiltonian $H_{e-ph}$ between these states, $A_i(E)=-\frac{1}{\pi}Im G_i(E)$ is the spectral function, $G_i(E) = (E - \epsilon_i -\Sigma)^{-1}$ is the Green's function. In the limit of negligible lifetime broadening $\gamma\to0$, 
Eq.~(\ref{eq:phonon-assist}) is reduced to the Fermi's golden rule, $\Gamma_{ph,i\to j}\propto N(\omega(k))\delta(\Delta\epsilon-\hbar\omega)$, where $\Delta\epsilon=\epsilon_j-\epsilon_i$. The difference between localized state energies is supplied by a phonon, Fig.~\ref{fig:4}(a). Consequently, hopping is suppressed at $T<\Delta\epsilon/k_B$ due to phonon freeze-out. 

For finite $\gamma$, $A_i(E)=\gamma/[E-\epsilon_i-\Sigma)^2+\gamma^2]$. In the limit $k_BT<\Delta\epsilon$, the sum in Eq.~(\ref{eq:phonon-assist}) is dominated by the low-frequency phonons with populations $N(\omega)=k_BT/\hbar\omega$, then $\Gamma_{ph,i\to j}=Tg$, with temperature-independent $g(\Delta\epsilon,\gamma)$ determined by the sum in Eq.~(\ref{eq:phonon-assist}). Phonon-assisted hopping is still suppressed at $T<\Delta\epsilon/k_B$, but much less than for $\gamma=0$. 

We identify another mechanism of hopping which does freeze out at $T=0$. A finite lifetime $\tau$ of state $i$ describes its decay into other electronic states, which generally have a finite amplitude on the state $j$ quasi-localized on the neighboring site, i.e., the matrix element $t_{ij}=\langle i|H_{e-e}|j\rangle$ of e-e interaction between these states is finite. The rate of hopping mediated by e-e interaction,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:electron-assist}
\Gamma_{e-e,i\to j} = \frac{2\pi}{\hbar}\sum_{E}|t_{ij}|^2A_i(E)A_j(E),
\end{equation}
is determined by the overlap between their spectral functions. If thermal effects on the spectral functions can be neglected, it is temperature independent. Energy conservation is satisfied due to the transfer of energy between the localized states and other electron states, via the same interaction mechanism that results in finite-lifetime broadening, Fig.~\ref{fig:4}(b).

The total hopping rate is 
$\Gamma_{i\to j}(T)=\Gamma_{ph}+\Gamma_{e-e}$. As the hopping process defines the effective diffusion rate, i.e. electron mobility, the temperature dependence of resistivity can be approximated by $\rho(T)\propto(\Gamma_{ph}(T_0)T/T_0+\Gamma_{e-e})^{-1}$, where $T_0$ is some reference temperature. This provides a good approximation for the temperature-dependent resistivities of $\beta$-Ta and all nitrogen-doped Ta films, Fig.~\ref{fig:4}(c). The ratio $\Gamma_{e-e}/\Gamma_{ph}(T_0)$ extracted from these fittings decreases with increasing nitrogen doping, which can be attributed to increasing contribution of disorder to localization reducing the overlap between quasi-localized states. Nevertheless, even at $T=300$~K, hopping mediated by e-e interaction dominates over ph-mediated hopping for all except the highest studied nitrogen doping, explaining the absence of signatures of phonon-mediated hopping in our SN measurements. 

\textbf{Summary.} Our shot noise measurements in  ultrashort nanojunctions of nitrogen-doped Ta revealed electron thermalization over the length not exceeding $8$~nm at $4.2$~K, indicating a short single-particle lifetime due to electron-electron interactions. Analysis shows that the latter can mediate hopping in a manner somewhat similar to the phonon-mediated hopping, explaining the `failed" insulator behaviors characterized by non-divergent increase of resistivity with decreasing temperature.

We present the first, to our knowledge, microscopic interpretation for the non-divergent negative temperature coefficient in ``failed" insulators. According to our analysis, transport in these materials involves both phonon- and electron interaction-mediated hopping of quasi-localized electronic states. In contrast to phonon-assisted hopping, hopping mediated by electron interactions is not suppressed at cryogenic temperatures, avoiding resistivity divergence. Our model provides an excellent approximation for the temperature-dependent resistivities. The role of electron interactions increases with decreasing disorder, suggesting that interactions may be the mechanism of electron localization in weakly disordered ``failed" insulators such as pure $\beta$-Ta. These materials may thus realize a Hund's metal state, a correlated non-Fermi liquid stabilized by electron interactions~\cite{osti_1694387}. They may be related to hydrodynamic metals where scattering is also dominated by interactions~\cite{Fritz2024}. This connection may be manifested in transport propeties in clean failed insulators at deep cryogenic temperatures, warranting further studies.

Momentum and energy transfer in interaction-mediated hopping may be facilitated by a many-particle electron fluid akin to incoherent Cooper pair condensate, as hypothesized for thin ``failed" insulator films near superconductor-to-insulator transition~\cite{Breznay2017,yang2019intermediate,zhang2022anomalous}. 
%This possibility is supported by the anomalously large shot noise recently observed in tunnel junctions based on nitrogen-doped Ta similar to LD-Ta studied in our work~\cite{zhang2025}. The Fano factors were shown to cluster around even multiples of values expected for single-electron tunneling, indicating the presence incoherent (finite-momentum) Cooper pairs consistent with the quasi-localized electron state. 
This possibility warrants further studies by electronic and optical techniques, and theoretical modelling of many-electron effects in these materials.

In spin-orbitronics, $\beta$-Ta is known as one of the most efficient spin Hall effect (SHE) sources, which has been traditionally attributed to its spin-orbit-coupled band structure~\cite{liu2012spin}. The effects of charge localization and strong interactions demonstrated by our study are presently unexplored in the context of SHE. Intriguingly, alloying of conventional Fermi liquids results in a relatively abrupt and significant enhancement of SHE on approaching the Ioffe-Regel regime  corresponding to the onset of the ``failed insulator" state~\cite{Shashank2025,liu2025enhancingzspingeneration}.

