\subsection{Goos–Hänchen shifts}
It is widely known in optics that when a light beam is fully reflected at the interface of two dielectric media, it experiences a lateral displacement from its original incident position. This phenomenon is known as the Goos–Hänchen (GH) shift \cite{Goos1947EinNU}. %Theoretical development of this effect is now enriching in studying scattering in electronic interface\cite{ling2021beam,Yu_2019,PhysRevLett.106.176802,PhysRevB.77.073307,Sharma_2011,zhou2022anisotropic} and spin waves systems \cite{LEE2023170465,ZhenYu2023TopologicalSA,lee2023scattering} using a standard quantum scattering approach \cite{PhysRevLett.102.146804,PhysRevLett.121.176602}. 
An analogous lateral shift has also been recently observed in the reflected quasiparticle (hole) beam during the Andreev reflection process at junctions, exhibiting distinct characteristics for both pseudospin-$\frac{1}{2}$ and pseudospin-$1$ fermions\cite{PhysRevB.101.235417,PhysRevB.109.035432,PhysRevB.98.075151,PhysRevB.105.085415}.   
%Some recent works on the GH shift focus primarily on AR processes and showed that similar lateral shifts can also be seen in reflected quasi-particle (hole) beams from the junction with distinguishable characteristics both in pseudospin-$\frac{1}{2}$ and pseudospin-$1$ fermions\cite{PhysRevB.101.235417,PhysRevB.109.035432,PhysRevB.98.075151,PhysRevB.105.085415}. In our setup, we observe that GH shift is significantly enhanced in the presence of quasi-flat bands. This enhancement arises due to the resonance wavelength over a wide range of incidence angles \cite{PhysRevB.110.035420}. Consequently, these shifts strongly influence the dispersion relations of confined waveguide modes, which modify the transport channels through the junction.



To numerically simulate GH shifts in our setup, we consider Gaussian wave packets to represent both electron-like and hole-like beams. Following Refs.~\onlinecite{PhysRevB.101.235417, PhysRevB.105.085415}, we directly compute the GH shift from the reflection amplitudes 
\begin{equation}
    \delta y_{e(h)}=-\frac{\partial \arg[r(r_A)]}{\partial k_y},
    \label{13}
\end{equation}
where $\delta y_{e(h)}$ is the lateral GH shift in the $y$-direction for electron and hole reflections respectively, for an incident electronic beam centered around wave vector ($k_x,k_y$). Here, $r$ and $r_A$ are electron reflection and AR amplitude, respectively. 
%\SM{ In our setup, we observe that the GH shift ($\delta y_{h}$) is significantly enhanced in the presence of quasi‑flat bands. This enhancement arises due to the resonance condition being met over a wide range of incidence angles \cite{PhysRevB.110.035420} and the directional asymmetry of the Fermi contours at the NS interface along the transverse ($k_y$) momentum component; see Appendix~\ref{appenD} for details.}

