\subsection{Sample Efficiency Analysis}
The primary objective of our proposed method is to enhance the sample efficiency of reinforcement learning by incorporating the model dynamics. Greater sample efficiency signifies that the model can achieve stronger learning performance with less data. To highlight the sample efficiency of our model, we conducted further analysis of the training data and generated two visual representations.

Figure \ref{fig:sample_efficiency}a illustrates the percentage of environment steps required for different algorithms to reach 80\% of the optimal return across six environments. As observed,  in all tasks except the Pendulum task, where Q-STAC and S\textsuperscript{2}AC require similar environment steps, Q-STAC consistently requires fewer environment interactions to achieve satisfactory task performance. In the reaching task, Q-STAC requires only 68.8\% timesteps to reach 80\% optimal return, while S\textsuperscript{2}AC needs 96\%, and PPO, TD3, and SAC failed. The results indicate that incorporating model-based knowledge can constrain robotic arm movements, thus reducing the probability of collisions with obstacles and achieving superior performance. Moreover, in the pick and place task, none of the other methods reached 80\% of the optimal return, suggesting that our approach can find better solutions in complex control problems.

Figure \ref{fig:sample_efficiency}b presents a box plot of the normalized mean return values over the first 50 training episodes for each algorithm for all tasks. The results demonstrate that Q-STAC exhibits the highest mean and maximum values compared to other algorithms, suggesting that Q-STAC maintains a leading position early in the training process. Overall, Q-STAC outperforms other algorithms in terms of sample efficiency. While S\textsuperscript{2}AC and SAC demonstrate competitive performance in relatively simple tasks, they generally fall short compared to Q-STAC. The on-policy PPO algorithm exhibits the lowest sample efficiency among all evaluated methods.

