The domain of  integration in the definition of $FB_{a, b, c}(z)$ splits into the disjoint sets \begin{align*} \mathcal M_1 = \{ ( c^\prime , d^\prime) : \, a < d^\prime - c^\prime < c/2 ; d^\prime \in [ 0 , b/2 ] \} \\ \mathcal M_2 =  \{ ( c^\prime , d^\prime) : c/2  < d^\prime - c^\prime < c ; d^\prime \in [ 0 , b/2 ] \} . \end{align*} 
The integral over $ \mathcal M_1 $ is $ FB_{a, b, \frac{c}{2}}(z)$ and the integral over $ \mathcal M_2 $ is estimated above by $ FB_{a, b, \frac{c}{2}}(z)$ just because $ \eta ( -	d^\prime , -c_1 , c_1 , d^\prime ) \le \eta ( -	d^\prime , -c_2 , c_2 , d^\prime ) $ whenever $ c/2  < d^\prime - c_1 < c $, $ a < d^\prime - c_2 < c/2 $. Since the denominators in the expectations in the integrands are $ \asymp d $ in both integrals, the result follows. 
