\begin{abstract}
% Enterprise systems, like business automation tools and LLM-powered AI agents, need standardized, machine-readable information for interacting with external APIs. 
% For AI agents, interacting with an API as a tool requires information such as description of the API and all of the request parameters and responses along with rich natural language descriptions. Often, such information is present in human-readable API documentation web pages. 
AI agents and business automation tools interacting with external web services require standardized, machine-readable information about their APIs in the form of API specifications. However, the information about APIs available online is often presented as unstructured, free-form HTML documentation, requiring external users to spend significant time manually converting it into a structured format.
% APIs, or Application Programming Interfaces, are the backbone of modern web services enabling seamless communication between different software systems, and powering digital innovation through their ease of use and standardization. They have and continue to be at the core of enterprise integration fueling business automation use cases. With the advent of chat bots, natural language queries to invoke APIs have become the norm, and with the surge of AI agents, APIs once again become the interface to tools used by agents. Open API Specifications (OAS) are critical for enabling both automation and agents to interact with APIs. Both use cases tend to interact with external enterprise services where specifications are often not available and require a large amount of manual effort to reverse engineer from available documentation.
%AI agents ingest API specifications as their tool catalog and rapidly interact and achieve the agent's intent. An Open API Specification, or OAS, becomes critical to facilitate these scenarios, yet is not always readily available for enterprise tools. 
% To address this, we introduce \productname{}, a novel framework that large language models to convert diverse API documentation into a consistent machine-readable API specifications through a carefully crafted pipeline.
To address this, we introduce \productname{}, a novel framework that transforms long and diverse API documentation pages into consistent, machine-readable API specifications. This is achieved through a carefully crafted pipeline that integrates large language models and rule-based algorithms which are guided by domain knowledge of the structure of documentation webpages.
Our experiments demonstrate that \productname{} generalizes well across hundreds of APIs, and produces valid OpenAPI specifications that encapsulate most of the information from the original documentation.
\productname{} has been successfully implemented in an enterprise environment, saving thousands of hours of manual effort and making hundreds of complex enterprise APIs accessible as tools for LLMs.

% \productname{} has been successfully deployed in an enterprise setting to enable natural language conversations to invoke APIs. It has saved thousands of hours of manual effort and has made hundreds of complex enterprise APIs available as tools for LLMs.
%In the digital era, most applications comprise multiple services or tools that communicate via APIs. 
% Unfortunately, many APIs lack standardized documentation, thereby hindering scalable utilization.
%Unfortunately, many APIs are only documented online with diverse formats.  This is a problem, since consuming APIs robustly at scale requires standardized specifications.
% In the digital era, the widespread use of APIs is evident.
% However, scalable utilization of APIs poses a challenge due to a lack of standardized documentation. 
% commonly in the form of specifications. 
%This underscores the need for automatic tools to facilitate API consumption. One promising solution is to transform the abundant online documentation into API specifications.
%Previous efforts using rule-based methods have struggled to generalize across various types of documentation. This paper introduces \productname{}, a novel framework that leverages large language models to generate OpenAPI Specifications from diverse API documentation through a carefully crafted pipeline including a UI platform for final processing.
%Our experiments demonstrate that \productname{} generalizes well across hundreds of APIs, and produces valid specifications that encapsulate most of the information from the original documentation. By creating a standardized format for numerous APIs, \productname{} streamlines large-scale API consumption and facilitates the integration of tools into large language models.
% While previous attempts have been made using rule-based methods, these approaches encountered difficulties in generalizing across diverse documentation. 
% This paper introduces \productname{}, a comprehensive framework that utilizes large language models (LLMs) to generate OpenAPI Specifications from diverse API documentation through a carefully crafted pipeline. 
% Through a set of experiments we show that \productname{} is capable of generalizing across hundreds of APIs, while producing valid specification containing most of the information from the documentation. By creating a standardized format for numerous APIs, \productname{} aids in streamlining integration processes within API orchestrating systems and facilitating the incorporation of tools into LLMs. 
% The paper explores \productname{}'s methodology, supported by empirical evidence and case studies.
%, demonstrating its efficacy through LLM capabilities.
\end{abstract}


