\begin{abstract}
Training native 3D texture generative models remains a fundamental yet challenging problem, largely due to the limited availability of large-scale, high-quality 3D texture datasets. This scarcity hinders generalization to real-world scenarios. To address this, most existing methods finetune foundation image generative models to exploit their learned visual priors. However, these approaches typically generate only multi-view images and rely on post-processing to produce UV texture maps—an essential representation in modern graphics pipelines. Such two-stage pipelines often suffer from error accumulation and spatial inconsistencies across the 3D surface.  
In this paper, we introduce \textbf{SeqTex}, a novel end-to-end framework that leverages the visual knowledge encoded in pretrained video foundation models to directly generate complete UV texture maps. Unlike previous methods that model the distribution of UV textures in isolation, \textbf{SeqTex} reformulates the task as a sequence generation problem, enabling the model to learn the joint distribution of multi-view renderings and UV textures. This design effectively transfers the consistent image-space priors from video foundation models into the UV domain.
To further enhance performance, we propose several architectural innovations: a decoupled multi-view and UV branch design, geometry-informed attention to guide cross-domain feature alignment, and adaptive token resolution to preserve fine texture details while maintaining computational efficiency. Together, these components allow \textbf{SeqTex} to fully utilize pretrained video priors and synthesize high-fidelity UV texture maps without the need for post-processing.
Extensive experiments show that SeqTex achieves state-of-the-art performance on both image-conditioned and text-conditioned 3D texture generation tasks, with superior 3D consistency, texture-geometry alignment, and real-world generalization. Our project page is \href{https://yuanze1024.github.io/SeqTex/}{https://yuanze1024.github.io/SeqTex/}.

%Training native 3D texture generative models is a fundamental problem that has attracted increased attention in the research community, but the scarcity of large-scale texture datasets makes it difficult for trained models to generalize well to real-world scenarios. Most texture methods address this issue by finetuning foundation image generative models to leverage their learned visual priors. However, finetuned models can only produce multi-view images and require post-processing to generate UV texture maps, which are widely adopted in modern graphics pipelines. This two-stage paradigm suffers from error accumulation and spatial incoherence on the 3D surface. In this paper, we propose SeqTex, a novel method that leverages knowledge from pretrained video diffusion models to generate complete UV texture maps in an end-to-end manner. Our key insight is that, unlike previous approaches that train generative models solely on the distribution of UV texture maps, our framework repurposes a video diffusion model to learn the joint distribution of multi-view images and UV textures. This allows the model to harness the consistent image-space priors encoded in video diffusion models and effectively transfer them into the UV domain. Furthermore, we introduce several architectural innovations—a decoupled MV-UV branch design, geometry-informed attention, and adaptive token resolution—which collectively enable effective learning while maintaining high computational efficiency and preserving the capabilities of the pretrained model. As a result, our approach fully exploits the rich visual priors present in video models and directly synthesizes complete UV texture maps without any post-processing. Extensive experiments demonstrate that SeqTex achieves state-of-the-art results on both image- and text-conditioned 3D texture generation, with superior 3D consistency, texture-geometry alignment, and real-world generalization.
\end{abstract}


