\section{Branched coarse coverings}\label{joopwbergregrfwrf}

For a $G$-set $X$   we consider the $G$-set
$G\times X$ with the diagonal $G$-action (involving the left-multiplication on $G$) and the additional $G$-action by right-multiplication on $G$. We consider the map
$$f:G\times X\to X\ , \quad f(g,x):=g^{-1}x\ .$$
It intertwines the additional $G$-action with the given $G$-action on $X$, and the diagonal $G$-action with the trivial $G$-action on $X$.

For   an entourage $W$    of $X$ we define the  entourage 
$$W_{G}:=f^{-1}(W)\cap \pr_{G}^{-1}(\diag(G)) $$
%
%\{((l,lx),(l,ly)) \mid l\in G\ , (x,y)\in W\}$$
of  $G\times X$
which is invariant for the diagonal $G$-action and satisfies 
 satisfies $f(W_{G})=W$. 
 If $X$ has a  coarse structure, then we equip $G\times X$ with the  coarse structure 
 generated by the entourages $W_{G}$ for all coarse  entourages $W$ of $X$.
  If the coarse structure  on $X$ is preserved by the $G$-action, then the additional $G$-action preserves
  %% $((gb,gx),(gb,gy))=((gb,gbb^{-1}x),(gby,gbb^{-1}y))$
  %% use that $\{(b^{-1}x,b^{-1}y)\mid (x,y)\in U\}$ is $bU$, again a coarse entourage
  %% 
  the coarse structure on $G\times X$. % and
 % the components $\{g\}\times X$ of $G\times X$  all have the coarse structure of $X$.

If $X$ has a bornology, then we equip $G\times X$ with the bornology generated by
the subsets $F\times B$ for finite subsets $F$ of $G$ and bounded subsets $B$ of $X$.
If the bornology on $X$ is preserved by $G$, then the map $f$ is bornological with locally finite fibres, but not proper in general.

If $X$ is a   bornological coarse space with $G$-action, then 
the structures on $G\times X$  defined above  turn  this set into a   $G$-bornological coarse space  which we will denote by $G\ltimes X$.  
 We thus get a functor
 $$G\ltimes -:\Fun(BG,\BC)\to \Fun(BG,G\BC)\ .$$
% The map $f$ is the component of a natural transformation $G\ltimes -\to \id$.


\begin{ex}
If $X$ is in $G\BC$, then $f:G\ltimes X\to X$ is a bounded covering in the sense of \cite{trans}.
Indeed, the map $(g,x)\to (g,g^{-1}x)$ induces an isomorphism
$G\ltimes X\cong G_{\min,\min}\otimes X$ under  which $f$ becomes the projection onto the second factor. \hB
\end{ex}

