\subsection{Step 2: aggregating local tests into a global test}
\label{section::2-3}


Recall that we break down the original problem in two stages: from a global test to local tests, one for each row; then, within each row, we further break down the local test by partitioning $\Itesti$ into disjoint subsets. We now aggregate the results of the local tests to devise a global rejection set with valid FDR control for predicting all missing links. To this end, we leverage the e-BH procedure \citep{wang2022false, ren2024derandomised}.

The e-BH procedure admits a notable advantage that its validity  requires only that each individual e-values satisfies $E(e_{i,j})\leq 1$, without any additional assumptions on their dependence structure.
The central idea of our global test is to first rescale the binary test decision functions from the local tests into valid e-values.
By employing the leave-one-out analysis of the BH procedure in \citet{ferreira2006}, we can show that $E\big[ \mathbbm{1}\{ j\in \cR_{\rm BH}^{i_0,k_0}(\alphaBH) \}/\{|\cR_{\rm BH}^{i_0,k_0}(\alphaBH)| \vee 1\}\big] \le \alphaBH/|\Itesti(k_0)| $, for each $j$.
Therefore, 
\begin{align}
    e_{i_0,j} := \frac{|\Itesti(k_0)| \cdot \mathbbm{1}\{ j\in \cR_{\rm BH}^{i_0,k_0}(\alphaBH) \}}{\{|\cR_{\rm BH}^{i_0,k_0}(\alphaBH)| \vee 1\}\cdot \alphaBH},
    \quad
    \text{for } j \in \Itesti(k_0)
    \label{equation::compute-e-value}
\end{align}
is a valid e-value, namely, $ E(e_{i_0,j})\leq 1 $ under the null $H_{i_0,j}$ in \eqref{def::hypothesis-testing-local}.
Then, by separately applying the e-BH procedure to $\{e_{i_0,j}, j \in \Itesti(k_0)\}$, one could obtain a local test for each row $i_0$ with the FDR controlled at level $\alpha_{\rm BH}$.
But here, we would directly construct a global test using all $\{e_{i,j}:(i,j) \in \Dtest\}$. To this end, we sort all e-values $\{e_{i,j}:(i,j) \in \Dtest\}$ in descending order: $e_{(1)} \geq \cdots \geq  e_{(|\Dtest|)}$. Then, for any given $\alphaeBH>0$, set
\begin{align}
    \wh k
    :=
    \max\left\{
        k:
        1\leq k\leq |\Dtest|,
        e_{(k)} \geq \frac{|\Dtest|}{\alphaeBH k}
    \right\},
    \quad
    \text{ or }~ \wh k = 0 \text{ if the set is empty},
    \label{eqation::compute-hat-k}
\end{align}
and reject all $H_{i,j}$'s with e-values $e_{i,j}\geq e_{(\wh k)}$.
The procedure fails to reject any hypotheses when $\wh k = 0$.
This rejection rule controls FDR of \eqref{def::hypothesis-testing} at the level $\alphaeBH$.

To mitigate variations arising from the random selection of $\Icalibi$ and $\Itraini$, we further adopt a derandomisation procedure \citep{ren2024derandomised}.
This builds on the property that the average of multiple e-values remains a valid e-value \citep{vovk2021values}.
For each row $i_0$ and $j \in \Itesti(k_0)$, we repeat Algorithm \ref{algorithm::sample-local-test} for $m_{i_0}$ times and compute the e-values $ e_{i_0,j}^{(1)},\ldots, e_{i_0,j}^{(m_{i_0})}$. Then we derandomise the e-values as follows:
\begin{align}
    \bar{e}_{i_0,j}
    :=
    \frac1{m_{i_0}}
    \sum_{l=1}^{m_{i_0}}
    e_{i_0,j}^{(l)},
    ~~
    \text{where } j \in \Itesti(k_0).
    \label{equation::compute-e-value-avg}
\end{align}
Finally, we apply the e-BH procedure to all $\bar e_{i,j}$'s as in \eqref{eqation::compute-hat-k}, using them in the same manner as $e_{i,j}$ in the original algorithm described above. This yields our method {\tt clp}, summarised in Algorithm \ref{algorithm::BH-EBH-framework}, which enjoys finite-sample valid FDR control as established in Theorem \ref{thm:emp-fdr-global}.

\begin{algorithm}[h!]
\caption{{\tt clp} method via e-value derandomisation}
\label{algorithm::BH-EBH-framework}

    \KwIn{ Data $\big\{A\circ M^c, M\big\}$, 
    $\{c_{i,j}\}_{(i,j) \in \Dtest}$, 
    calibration size $r_0 \ge C/\alphaBH$, the number of derandomisations $m_{i_0}$ for $i_0 \in [n]$,
    and $\alphaBH, \alphaeBH \in (0, 1)$.}
    \For{$i_0 \in [n]$ where $|\Itesti|>0$ }{

       Randomly construct $\Icalibi$ and partition $\Itesti$ into $k = \lceil \abs{\Itesti} / r_1 \rceil$ approximately equal subsets, denote by $\Itesti(k_0)$, where $|\Itesti(k_0)| \leq r_1 := \lfloor \abs{\Icalibi}/r_0\rfloor$ for $k_0\in [k]$ \;

        \For{$k_0\in [k] $ }{
        Select $\Itesti(k_0)$ as the target test set\;

        \For{$m\in [m_{i_0}] $ }{
        Randomly construct $\Icalibi$ and $\Itraini$\;
        Run Algorithm \ref{algorithm::sample-local-test}
        and compute the e-values $\{e_{i_0,j}^{(m)}:j \in \Itesti(k_0)\}$ via \eqref{equation::compute-e-value}\;
       }
    Compute the e-values $\big\{\bar{e}_{i_0,j}:j \in \Itesti(k_0)\big\}$ using \eqref{equation::compute-e-value-avg}\;
    } 
    }

    Record e-values $\{\bar{e}_{i,j}:(i,j) \in \Dtest\}$ and compute $\wh k$ using \eqref{eqation::compute-hat-k}\;

    \KwOut{The rejection set $\cR_{\rm eBH}:=\left\{(i,j) \in \Dtest:\bar{e}_{i,j} \geq |\Dtest| / (\alphaeBH \wh k )\right\}$.}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{theorem}[FDR control of {\tt clp} for testing \eqref{def::hypothesis-testing}]
\label{thm:emp-fdr-global} 
    Under the conditions of Theorem \ref{thm:local-FDR-split}, given any $\alphaeBH\in (0,1)$ and for any choice of $\alphaBH \in (0,1)$, the rejection set produced by Algorithm \ref{algorithm::BH-EBH-framework} satisfies FDR $\leq \alphaeBH$.
\end{theorem}


We make two important remarks.
First, our method {\tt clp} accommodates arbitrary missing mechanism, provided Assumption \ref{assumption::rm} is satisfied.
Examples include: 1) random but non-uniform missingness \citep{gui2023conformalized}; 2) structured missingness, such as block and staggered missingness commonly encountered in panel data \citep{athey2021matrix,xiong2023large}.
Second, as shown in Theorem \ref{thm:emp-fdr-global}, {\tt clp} achieves valid FDR control for any choice of $\alphaBH$, while \cite{ren2024derandomised} recommend setting $\alphaBH=\alphaeBH/2$ for better powers.

We note that our method may exhibit some conservativeness in practice.
By \eqref{equation::compute-e-value}, the maximum possible value of $e_{i_0,j}$ for $j \in \Itesti(k_0)$ is $|\Itesti(k_0)|/\alphaBH$. Thus, when $|\Itesti(k_0)|$ is small due to extensive data-splitting, the resulting e-values may be lowered, limiting the number of rejections under the e-BH procedure in \eqref{eqation::compute-hat-k}.
To alleviate this effect, we introduce an inflation factor to adjust each e-value $e_{i,j}$ and increase the likelihood of rejection. Numerical studies in Section \ref{section::numerical-studies} explore distinct choices of this inflation factor and suggest that using a factor of $1/\alphaBH$ effectively enhances  power while maintaining well-controlled FDR.
A more rigorous theoretical characterisation of this practically effective factor remains an intriguing yet challenging direction for future work.


Computing the dissimilarity measure in \eqref{def::d_{j_1,j_2}} incurs a computational burden due to the evaluation of all terms $\hat d_{j_1,j_2,j}$ in \eqref{def::d_{j_1,j_2,j}}. 
Consequently, the overall computational complexity of {\tt clp} is $O\big(m|\Itraini|^2|\Icalibi|\big)$, where $m$ is the total number of missing entries, assuming that $|\Itraini|$ (and $|\Icalibi|$) are of the same order across all $i_0 \in [n]$. Specifically, the computation of \eqref{def::d_{j_1,j_2,j}} and \eqref{def::d_{j_1,j_2}} incurs a cost of $O\big(|\Itraini|\big)$, while the aggregation step in \eqref{method::A-estimate} contributes an additional $O\big(|\Icalibi|)$. We can accelerate our method by reducing the sizes of $\Itraini$ and $\Icalibi$; for instance, setting $\Itraini\asymp\Icalibi=O(1)$ reduces the overall complexity to $O(m)$. However, this may lead to less accurate estimation of the dissimilarity measure, potentially reducing the power.


\subsection{Extensions and comparisons}
While our proposed framework has been detailed for directed networks, it extends naturally to bipartite and undirected networks with minimal modifications. 
The adaptation of our method to bipartite networks is straightforward, owing to the flexible exchangeability of bipartite networks. 
That is, we can use more rows to construct $\wh A_{i_0,j}$ and the associated $p$-values. The extension to undirected networks requires extra care due to the symmetry constraints $A_{i,j}=A_{j,i}$ and $M_{i,j}=M_{j,i}$. Yet, the solution turns out to be remarkably simple.
We first apply our method in Algorithms \ref{algorithm::sample-local-test} and \ref{algorithm::BH-EBH-framework}, until we obtain $\bar e_{i,j}$'s at Line 9 of Algorithm \ref{algorithm::BH-EBH-framework}.
Thereafter, we apply the rest of Algorithm \ref{algorithm::BH-EBH-framework}, the e-BH procedure, only to the upper triangular part, that is, $\{\bar e_{i,j}: (i,j)\in \Dtest \text{ and }i<j\}$, rather than the full set $\{\bar e_{i,j}: (i,j)\in \Dtest\}$. Formal algorithmic descriptions (Algorithms \ref{algorithm::sample-local-test-und} and \ref{algorithm::sample-local-test-bipartite}) and theoretical justifications for both extensions are provided in Section \ref{supp-section::bipartitie-and-undirected} of the Supplementary Material.


We conclude this section by comparing our method with existing works.
\citet{gui2023conformalized} addresses arbitrary $A$ under a missing-at-random mechanism, requiring knowledge or accurately estimates of the ratios between missing and non-missing probabilities $q_{i,j}/(1-q_{i,j})$, which are undefined when $q_{i,j}=1$. 
In contrast, we consider with arbitrary missingness patterns $M$ that are independent of $A$, and leverage the latent graphon structure in $A$ to reinstate the exchangeability. This allows our method to handle a much broader class of missing mechanisms.
Overall, the two approaches rely on different assumptions and do not subsume each other. More recently, \citet{marandon2024conformal, blanchard2024fdr} introduced a novel approach to FDR control by framing link prediction as a classification problem. 
While innovative, this formulation inherently limits their methods to unweighted networks and they assume that the missingness rates can take only two distinct values, depending on whether an entry is $0$ or $1$. In contrast, our method applies to both weighted and unweighted networks and allows fully heterogeneous, unknown missingness rates, offering greater flexibility in practical applications.



