\section{Simulations}
\label{section::numerical-studies}

\subsection{Simulation 1: tuning our method}
\label{section::numerical-studies-1}


We generate networks following the distribution $A_{i,j} = f(\xi_i,\xi_j) + \epsilon_{i,j}$, where $\epsilon_{i,j} \sim \mathrm{Uniform}[-0.1,0.1]$.
% We generate networks from $A_{i,j} = f(\xi_i,\xi_j) + \epsilon_{i,j}$, where $\epsilon_{i,j} \sim \mathrm{Uniform}[-0.1,0.1]$.
Three graphons are considered to cover a wide range of scenarios: 
\begin{itemize}
    \item Setting 1 (low-rank and smooth): $f_1(\xi_i,\xi_j) = \xi_i^3 + 2\xi_j^3$; 

    \item Setting 2 (high-rank): $f_2(\xi_i,\xi_j) = \max(\xi_i, \xi_j)^{2/3}\cos[0.1/\{(2 \xi_i-1/2)^3 + (\xi_j-1/2)^3 + 0.01\}]$;

    \item Setting 3 (non-smooth): $f_3(\xi_i,\xi_j) =  (3 \xi_i^2 + \xi_j^2) \cdot \cos\{{(2 \xi_i^4 + \xi_j^4)}^{-1}\}$.
\end{itemize}
We fix the network size at $n=200$ and set the proportion of missing links to $0.2$, resulting in an average of $3,980$ missing links. Specifically, we consider heterogeneous missing probabilities, where $q_{i,j}$'s are independently generated from $\mathrm{Uniform}[0,0.4]$.
We set the null hypotheses to be true, namely $c_{i,j} = A_{i,j}$ in \eqref{def::hypothesis-testing}, for $70$\% of entries.
For the remaining $30\%$, we set $c_{i,j} = A_{i,j} - 1.5$.
For each row $i_0$, all observed entries are randomly split into $\Itraini \cup \Icalibi$ with a ratio of $|\Itraini|:|\Icalibi|=2:3$, and we set $\alphaBH = \alphaeBH/2$ following \citet{ren2024derandomised}.
In the BH procedure, we choose $r_0=25$, as defined in the paragraph following \eqref{def::hypothesis-testing-local-sampleV}. We use the Gaussian kernel
$K(x) = \exp\big(-x^2/2\big)/\sqrt{2\pi}$ in \eqref{method::A-estimate} and derandomise local tests using $20$ repetitions in \eqref{equation::compute-e-value-avg}.

Recall that in \eqref{equation::compute-e-value}, we inflate the e-values by a factor of $c/\alphaBH$.
Here, we investigate the impact of $c\in\{0.5, 1, 1.5\}$.
Performance is evaluated using the FDR, approximated by averaged FDP, and power. Both metrics are assessed over $100$ experiments. We vary $\alphaeBH$ from $0.1$ to $0.3$.

\begin{figure}[h]
    \centering
    \vspace{1em}
    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{infl-c-shift-stderr-bound.pdf}
    \vspace{-2em}
    \caption{Empirical ratio of FDR and $\alphaeBH$, and empirical power under varying $\alphaBH$ and $\alphaeBH$. }
    \label{fig::infl-c}
\end{figure}

The results in Figure \ref{fig::infl-c} show that our method consistently controls the empirical FDR across a range of significance levels when $c=0.5$, and in most configurations when $c=1$. It also achieves desirable power results as $\alphaeBH$ increases. Overall, we recommend $c=1$ as it strike a satisfactory balance between FDR control and achieving decent power.



\subsection{Simulation 2: comparison with benchmark methods}

We compare our method ({\tt clp}) with conformal matrix completion ({\tt cmc}, \cite{gui2023conformalized}) and 
the link prediction method by \cite{marandon2024conformal} ({\tt dss}) via two sub-simulations for weighted and unweighted networks, respectively.
While {\tt cmc} applies to both settings, {\tt dss} is only applicable to unweighted networks.

For the first sub-simulation (weighted links), we adopt Settings 1--3 from Simulation 1 and fix $\alphaBH=0.1$ and $\alphaeBH=0.2$.
The threshold $c_{ij}$ is set as a specific quantile of the observed link weights, with quantile level $\kappa$ varying from $0.1$ to $0.5$. As noted earlier, we inflate the e-values in \eqref{equation::compute-e-value} by a factor of $1/\alpha_{\text{BH}}$. 
Note that {\tt cmc} only provides confidence intervals with guaranteed coverage rate, rather than p-values. Its underlying idea is that it computes the empirical quantiles of calibrated absolute errors as the population quantiles of absolute prediction errors, which is valid only for two-sided problems.
To adapt {\tt cmc} for our one-sided multiple testing \eqref{def::hypothesis-testing}, we modify it to compute corresponding p-values: we omit the absolute value when computing errors, then construct the empirical distribution function $\wh{F}_{\text{eCDF}}(\cdot)$ based on the calibrated errors $\wh{A}_{ij}-A_{ij}$. Finally, we compute p-values as $1-\wh{F}_{\text{eCDF}}(\wh{A}_{ij}-c_{ij}), (i,j)\in\Dtest$, and apply the BH procedure. 
Note that {\tt cmc} requires a hypothesised rank as input. In practice, we observe that the results of {\tt cmc} are robust to varying the hypothesised rank, as also noted by \citet{gui2023conformalized}. Accordingly, we use rank $r=2$ for {\tt cmc} across all settings for demonstration purposes.

\begin{figure}[h]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{compare-contin-stderr-bound-unif-c-quantile-k.pdf}
    \vspace{-2em}
    \caption{Comparisons of empirical FDRs and powers across different $c_{ij}$'s under Settings 1--3, with $\alphaBH=0.1$ and $\alphaeBH=0.2$. 
    }
    \label{fig::weight-unif-c}
\end{figure}

As shown in Figure \ref{fig::weight-unif-c}, while both {\tt clp} and {\tt cmc} control the FDR, {\tt clp} consistently achieves higher power across all settings. 
We note that {\tt cmc} is sensitive to the choice of $c_{i,j}$ values. In Section \ref{supp::additional-numerical-studies} of the Supplementary Material, we adopt the approach in Section \ref{section::numerical-studies-1} to select $c_{i,j}$ based on signal strength. Under such configuration, {\tt cmc} fails to control the FDR, whereas {\tt clp} continues to perform well.


To compare with \citet{marandon2024conformal}, we set up a second sub-simulation for unweighted networks, using a different data generating process from that employed for the weighted networks above. Specifically, we generate $A_{i,j} = \mathbbm{1}\{(\xi_i+\xi_j)/2 + \epsilon_{i,j}>t\}$, where $\epsilon_{i,j} \sim \mathrm{Uniform}[-0.25,0.25]$.
We set $c_{i,j}=0.5$ in \eqref{def::hypothesis-testing} and vary $t \in \{0,0.05,0.1,0.15,0.2\}$ to control the number of null and alternative hypotheses.
We consider heterogeneous missing probabilities, with $q_{i,j}$ independently drawn from $\mathrm{Uniform}[0,0.2]$, resulting in an average proportion of missing links of approximately $0.1$.
Here, we fix $\alphaBH=0.2$ and $\alphaeBH=0.2$, and use $r_0=50$ to improve estimation of $A_{i,j}$ for unweighted networks.

